From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qbtOS Developers <qbtos@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 23:20:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] serial: check that stdout device was bound

lists_bind_fdt() returns zero when no driver matches a node and clears the
output device pointer. serial_check_stdout() treated every zero return as a
successful bind and dereferenced the resulting NULL pointer.

This is observable on Raspberry Pi 4 after its firmware applies the serial
aliases and disable-bt overlay: U-Boot takes a synchronous abort during
serial_init before it can reach the console. Only inspect and probe the device
when lists_bind_fdt() actually returned one. The existing fallback search can
then select an available serial device.

Upstream: Not submitted; retained until the fix is verified against current U-Boot
Signed-off-by: qbtOS Developers <qbtos@example.invalid>
---
 drivers/serial/serial-uclass.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/serial/serial-uclass.c b/drivers/serial/serial-uclass.c
index c22a17f6f4..b9fdcb9b36 100644
--- a/drivers/serial/serial-uclass.c
+++ b/drivers/serial/serial-uclass.c
@@ -63,7 +63,7 @@ static int serial_check_stdout(const void *blob, struct udevice **devp)
 	 */
 	if (node > 0 && !lists_bind_fdt(gd->dm_root, offset_to_ofnode(node),
 					devp, NULL, false)) {
-		if (device_get_uclass_id(*devp) == UCLASS_SERIAL &&
+		if (*devp && device_get_uclass_id(*devp) == UCLASS_SERIAL &&
 		    !device_probe(*devp))
 			return 0;
 	}
